            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Johannes Vermeer</strong><br>\nJohannes Vermeer was born in 1632 in Delft, Netherlands. His father was a silk worker and later an art dealer, which gave young Vermeer exposure to the art world. Not much is known about his early training, but he was admitted to the Saint Luke's Guild as a master painter in 1653. His early works show a strong influence from other Dutch masters of the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Career and Work</strong><br>\nVermeer worked slowly and with great care, often using very expensive pigments. He was not widely known outside of Delft and had a relatively small output of paintings, around 36 of which survive today. His work was characterized by a nuanced understanding of light and shadow, detailed domestic scenes, and rich colors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Art of Light</strong><br>\nVermeer's paintings are celebrated for their brilliant use of light. He often painted indoor scenes where the light source comes from a window, creating a play of light and shadow across the subjects of his paintings. This mastery gives his works a tranquil and timeless quality that captivates viewers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
