          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Genres and Subjects</strong><br>\nVermeer's subject matter was varied, but he frequently depicted ordinary domestic life. He showed people engaged in everyday activities, such as reading letters, playing musical instruments, or in quiet reflection. This approach to genre painting was innovative in highlighting the serene beauty in common tasks and spaces.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Techniques and Style</strong><br>\nVermeer is known for his meticulous technique and precise style. He used the camera obscura, an early form of projector, to achieve accurate perspective in his compositions. His method involved applying several layers of paint, allowing each layer to dry before applying the next, contributing to the intense clarity and depth of his paintings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Hardships</strong><br>\nDespite his artistic brilliance, Vermeer's life was marked by financial difficulty. He lived with his wife and children in a house owned by his mother-in-law, struggling to sell his paintings. The economic downturn in the Netherlands during the 1670s further strained his finances, causing stress and hardship.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
