          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Family and Personal Life</strong><br>\nVermeer married Catharina Bolnes, a Catholic, and they had 15 children together, though not all survived to adulthood. His marriage brought him connections with higher social classes but did not significantly alleviate his financial woes. Personal details about his family life are sparse, as he tended to keep his private life out of the public eye.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nAfter his death in 1675, Vermeer fell into obscurity for centuries. It was not until the 19th century that his work was rediscovered by art historians and collectors. Today, he is regarded as one of the greatest painters of the Dutch Golden Age, and his works influence artists and captivate audiences worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vermeer in Modern Times</strong><br>\nIn modern times, Vermeer's work has gained considerable attention. Exhibitions of his paintings draw crowds eager to see his work firsthand. His depiction of light and everyday life continues to influence modern cinematography and photography, showing his enduring impact on the visual arts.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
